  <h2 id="choose">Which study planner should you choose?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>School student (Class 9–12) on Android:</strong> ClassTrack, for exam countdowns and board-exam revision plans. Pair it with our <a href="/guides/board-exam-study-timetable/">board exam timetable</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>School student on iPhone:</strong> MyStudyLife.</li>
    <li><strong>College student in India:</strong> ClassTrack, for photo/PDF timetable import, SGPA/CGPA and attendance.</li>
    <li><strong>College student in the US or UK who tracks GPA closely:</strong> Power Planner or MyStudyLife.</li>
    <li><strong>Entrance-exam aspirant (JEE, NEET, CUET, CAT):</strong> an AI study plan plus a daily timeline: ClassTrack for the plan, or Structured for hour-by-hour blocks.</li>
    <li><strong>Note-heavy university student:</strong> Notion for notes, with Google Calendar for classes.</li>
    <li><strong>Struggles with focus and procrastination:</strong> TickTick or Structured, for timers and streaks.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Not sure yet? Try one app for two full weeks before switching. Most students who “need a better planner” actually need to open the one they have every morning.</p>

  <h2 id="use">How to get results from any study planner</h2>
  <p>The app is only the container. These five habits are what make it work:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Enter everything once, at the start of term.</strong> Timetable, every known exam date and every assignment deadline. A planner with gaps is a planner you stop trusting.</li>
    <li><strong>Plan the week every Sunday.</strong> Ten minutes: check deadlines for the next 7–14 days and put study blocks in your calendar. Our guide to a <a href="/guides/weekly-schedule-for-students/">weekly student schedule</a> has examples.</li>
    <li><strong>Work backwards from exams.</strong> Split the syllabus into topics and give each one a slot, leaving the last week for past papers. See the <a href="/guides/study-plan-for-exams/">backward planning method</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Space your revision.</strong> Revisit each topic several times over days or weeks instead of once. In a study of over 1,300 people, the best gap between sessions for a test a week away was about 20–40% of the time left (Cepeda et al., 2008).</li>
    <li><strong>Make every block a test.</strong> End each study block with questions, flashcards or explaining from memory. Read more in <a href="/guides/evidence-based-study-techniques/">what the research says about study techniques</a>.</li>
  </ol>
  <blockquote>Want a plan right now, without an app? Enter your subjects and exam date in our free <a href="/tools/study-plan-generator/">study plan generator</a> and get a day-by-day timetable with revision built in.</blockquote>
</Guide>
